            <ArticleTitle>Snapshot of Oxidation of Thiolate by Diiodine: Stabilization of Intermediate by NH···S Hydrogen Bonds.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2187-2192</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acs.joc.7b00160</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ordinary thiolate (RS-) reacts with diiodine (I2) to afford an intermediate sulfenyl iodide (RSI) by releasing I-; RSI is readily converted to disulfide (RSSR) by a disproportionation reaction. In the case of thiolate Ar1S- containing very bulky acylamino groups forming NH···S hydrogen bonds, the crystal of the intermediate, [Ar1S-I-I]-, was obtained under usual conditions, and the structure was determined by X-ray diffraction analysis. The results show that the intramolecular NH···S hydrogen bonds stabilized the intermediate [Ar1S-I-I]-, consistent with theoretical calculations.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
